n\": \"Epistrophe repeats words at the end of successive clauses or sentences for rhetorical effect. Example: 'government of the people, by the people, for the people.'\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"rhetoric\",\r\n      \"repetition\",\r\n      \"epistrophe\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F306\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is Parallelism?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Using similar grammatical structures in a series for rhythm and balance\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Parallelism uses similar grammatical structures to create balance, rhythm, and emphasis. Example: 'I came, I saw, I conquered.' It makes writing more memorable and persuasive.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"rhetoric\",\r\n      \"structure\",\r\n      \"parallelism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F307\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is Antithesis?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Juxtaposition of contrasting ideas in balanced phrases\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Antithesis places contrasting ideas in parallel structures to emphasize opposition. Example: 'It was the best of times, it was the worst of times.' Creates dramatic contrast.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"rhetoric\",\r\n      \"contrast\",\r\n      \"antithesis\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F308\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is Chiasmus?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Inverted parallelism\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Chiasmus reverses the structure of parallel phrases for memorable effect. Example: JFK's 'Ask not what your country can do for you—ask what you can do for your country.'\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"rhetoric\",\r\n      \"structure\",\r\n      \"chiasmus\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F309\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a Metaphor?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Direct comparison between two unlike things without 'like' or 'as'\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"A metaphor makes a direct comparison between two unlike things to create vivid imagery. Example: 'Time is a thief.' It implies shared qualities without explicit comparison words.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"figurative\",\r\n      \"metaphor\",\r\n      \"comparison\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F310\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a Simile?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Comparison using 'like' or 'as'\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"A simile explicitly compares two unlike things using 'like' or 'as.' Example: 'Her eyes sparkled like diamonds.' Creates vivid imagery through direct comparison.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"figurative\",\r\n      \"simile\",\r\n      \"comparison\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F311\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is Personification?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Giving human qualities to non-human things\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Personification attributes human characteristics to animals, objects, or abstract concepts. Example: 'The wind whispered through the trees.' Makes writing more relatable and vivid.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"figurative\",\r\n      \"personification\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F312\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is Hyperbole?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Deliberate exaggeration for emphasis or humor\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Hyperbole uses extreme exaggeration to emphasize a point or create humor. Example: 'I've told you a million times!' Not meant to be taken literally.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"figurative\",\r\n      \"hyperbole\",\r\n      \"exaggeration\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F313\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is Understatement?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Deliberately downplaying significance for irony\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Understatement minimizes the importance of something, often for ironic or humorous effect. Example: Calling a hurricane 'a bit of bad weather.' Creates subtle emphasis through minimization.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"rhetoric\",\r\n      \"understatement\",\r\n      \"irony\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\