": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Why would a teacher compare Things Fall Apart by Chinua Achebe with early British colonial novels?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"To expose students to non-Western viewpoints and post-colonial themes\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Achebe's work reclaims narrative authority, showing literature's power to challenge dominant cultural perspectives. This comparison helps students understand colonialism from multiple viewpoints and recognize how literature shapes historical narratives.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"post-colonial literature\",\r\n      \"cultural perspectives\",\r\n      \"narrative authority\",\r\n      \"teaching literature\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F645\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is the primary purpose of Emily Brontë's use of multiple narrators and non-linear structure in Wuthering Heights?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"To present differing psychological perspectives that complicate moral judgment\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Brontë's framing devices (Lockwood and Nelly) invite readers to weigh conflicting viewpoints, deepening the novel's moral ambiguity. This narrative technique creates layers of interpretation and challenges readers to form their own moral conclusions.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"Wuthering Heights\",\r\n      \"Emily Brontë\",\r\n      \"narrative structure\",\r\n      \"multiple narrators\",\r\n      \"moral ambiguity\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F646\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"question\": \"How does Elizabeth Gaskell's North and South address industrialization and class conflict?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"It integrates social realism with moral sympathy to critique economic inequality\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Gaskell's realist approach humanizes labor issues and bridges moral and social commentary—an early example of industrial fiction. She combines detailed observation of working-class life with empathetic storytelling to awaken social conscience.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"Elizabeth Gaskell\",\r\n      \"North and South\",\r\n      \"industrialization\",\r\n      \"social realism\",\r\n      \"Victorian literature\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F647\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Why was Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre considered groundbreaking among nineteenth-century novels?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"It depicted a female protagonist whose moral and emotional autonomy challenge Victorian gender norms\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Jane's insistence on self-respect and conscience makes the novel an early feminist and psychological narrative. She refuses to compromise her values for love or social status, asserting her right to independence and moral agency.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"Charlotte Brontë\",\r\n      \"Jane Eyre\",\r\n      \"feminism\",\r\n      \"Victorian gender norms\",\r\n      \"female autonomy\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F648\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"question\": \"How does Emily Brontë's depiction of nature in Wuthering Heights differ from typical Romantic literature?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"It presents the natural world as indifferent or violent rather than purely inspiring\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Her wild moorland setting mirrors the intensity of human passion and cruelty—nature as emotional landscape, not idyllic refuge. Unlike Romantic idealization of nature as a source of transcendence, Brontë's nature reflects raw human emotion and conflict.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"Wuthering Heights\",\r\n      \"Emily Brontë\",\r\n      \"nature imagery\",\r\n      \"Romanticism\",\r\n      \"Gothic literature\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F649\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Why does Elizabeth Gaskell combine sentimental narrative techniques with journalistic observation in Mary Barton?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"To appeal to middle-class readers' empathy for working-class suffering\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Gaskell uses empathy and realism to awaken social conscience among her readership—hallmarks of early social-problem fiction. This blend makes the industrial struggles accessible and emotionally resonant to readers unfamiliar with factory life.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"Elizabeth Gaskell\",\r\n      \"Mary Barton\",\r\n      \"social conscience\",\r\n      \"social-problem fiction\",\r\n      \"Victorian literature\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F650\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"English syntax and sentence structure\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What makes the sentence 'They the park quickly across ran' incorrect?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"It violates English syntax by scrambling normal word order (subject–verb–object)\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Standard English syntax follows subject–verb–object order. The correct version would be 'They ran quickly across the park.' Scrambling word order creates ungrammatical sentences that violate English conventions.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"syntax\",\r\n      \"word order\",\r\n      \"sentence structure\",\r\n      \"grammar\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F651\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Parallel structure and sentence coherence\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is faulty parallel structure and why is 'She enjoys dancing, to swim, and playing tennis' incorrect?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Parallel structure requires consistent grammatical forms; this mixes gerunds with an infinitive\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Parallel structure means using the same grammatical pattern for items in a series. The correct version would be 'She enjoys dancing, swimming, and playing tennis' (all gerunds) or 'She likes to dance, to swim, and to play tennis' (all infinitives).\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"parallel structure\",\r\n      \"grammar\",\r\n      \"consistency\",\r\n      \"sentence structure\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F652\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Sentence fragments and complete sentences\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a sentence fragment and why is 'Because she was late to the meeting' incomplete?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"A fragment is a dependent clause without a main clause; this needs an independent clause to be complete\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"A sentence fragment lacks a complete thought. 'Because she was late to the meeting' is a dependent clause that needs a main clause, like 'Because she was late to the meeting, she missed the announcement.' Dependent clauses cannot stand alone.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"sentence fragments\",\r\n      \"dependent clauses\",\r\n      \"complete sentences\",\r\n      \"grammar\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F653\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Modifiers and clarity\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a misplaced modifier and what's wrong with 'The teacher handed the papers to the students on the desk'?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"'On the desk' incorrectly modifies 'students' instead of 'papers', creating confusion about what's on the desk\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"A misplaced modifier is positioned incorrectly, causing ambiguity or illogical meaning. The sentence suggests students are on the desk. The correct version would be 'The teacher handed the papers on the desk to the students' or 'The teacher handed to the students the papers on the desk.'\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"misplaced modifiers\",\r\n      \"clarity\",\r\n      \"grammar\",\r\n      \"sentence structure\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F654\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Structural ambiguity and coordination\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What makes 'She told him she loved her cat and so did he' structurally ambiguous?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"The coordination creates ambiguity: did he also tell her, or did he also love her cat?\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Structural ambiguity occurs when sentence structure allows multiple interpretations. 'So did he' could mean 'he also told her' or 'he also loved her cat.' Clear writing avoids such ambiguity through careful coordination and punctuation.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"structural ambiguity\",\r\n      \"coordination\",\r\n      \"clarity\",\r\n      \"sentence structure\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F655\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphology and word structure\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What are bound morphemes and why does 'unbelievable' contain two of them?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"'Un-' and '-able' are both bound morphemes that cannot stand alone; they attach to the free base 'believe'\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Bound morphemes are meaningful units that must attach to other morphemes. In 'unbelievable,' 'un-' (negation prefix) and '-able' (adjective-forming suffix) are bound to the free morpheme 'believe.' Neither can function independently as words.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"bound morphemes\",\r\n      \"word structure\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F656\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Inflectional vs. derivational morphology\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is inflectional morphology and why is 'walk → walked' an example?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Inflectional morphology changes grammatical function without changing core meaning; '-ed' marks past tense\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Inflectional morphemes modify grammatical features (tense, number, case) without creating new words or changing word class. 'Walked' is still a verb meaning 'to walk,' just in past tense. This differs from derivational morphology, which creates new words.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"inflectional morphology\",\r\n      \"grammar\",\r\n      \"tense\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F657\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Syntactic ambiguity\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Why is 'Visiting relatives can be exhausting' syntactically ambiguous?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"'Visiting relatives' can be a noun phrase (the act of visiting) or a participial phrase (relatives who visit)\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Syntactic ambiguity occurs when phrase structure allows multiple interpretations. This sentence could mean: (1) the act of visiting relatives is exhausting, or (2) relatives who are visiting you are exhausting. The phrase structure permits both readings.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"syntactic ambiguity\",\r\n      \"phrase structure\",\r\n      \"linguistics\",\r\n      \"grammar\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F658\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Derivational morphology\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is derivational morphology and why is 'happy → unhappy' an example?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Derivational morphology creates new words with changed meanings; 'un-' transforms the meaning of 'happy'\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Derivational morphemes create new words by changing meaning or word class. Adding 'un-' to 'happy' creates a word with opposite meaning. Unlike inflectional morphemes (which only add grammatical information), derivational changes are more fundamental and create distinct lexical items.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"derivational morphology\",\r\n      \"word formation\",\r\n      \"prefixes\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F659\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Semantic agreement\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is semantic agreement and why does 'The thunder laughed across the sky' violate it?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Semantic agreement requires logical relationships between subject and predicate; thunder cannot literally laugh\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Semantic agreement ensures that predicates are logically compatible with their subjects. Thunder (an atmospheric sound) cannot perform the human action of laughing—this is a semantic mismatch. While acceptable as figurative language (personification), it violates literal semantic agreement.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"semantic agreement\",\r\n      \"subject-predicate\",\r\n      \"personification\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F660\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Phonological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a phoneme?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Smallest sound unit that changes meaning\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Minimal sound unit that distinguishes words. Example: /k/ in 'cat' vs. /b/ in 'bat'.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"phoneme\",\r\n      \"phonology\",\r\n      \"sound\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F661\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Phonological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a grapheme?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Written letter(s) representing a sound\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Written symbol for a phoneme. Example: 'ph' in 'phone' = /f/ sound.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"grapheme\",\r\n      \"phonology\",\r\n      \"writing system\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F662\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Phonological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a digraph?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Two letters making one sound\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Letter pair representing single phoneme. Examples: 'sh' (ship), 'ch' (chair), 'th' (think).\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"digraph\",\r\n      \"phonology\",\r\n      \"spelling\",\r\n      \"literacy\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F663\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Phonological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is an allophone?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Variant pronunciation of same phoneme\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Different sounds of one phoneme based on context. Example: /p/ in 'spin' vs. 'pin' (aspirated).\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"allophone\",\r\n      \"phonology\",\r\n      \"pronunciation\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F664\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Phonological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is prosody?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Rhythm, stress, and intonation in speech\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"How pitch, rhythm, and stress convey meaning. Example: 'You're going?' (rising = question).\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"prosody\",\r\n      \"intonation\",\r\n      \"stress\",\r\n      \"phonology\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F665\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a morpheme?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Smallest unit of meaning\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Minimal meaningful unit. Example: 'unkindly' = un- (not) + kind + -ly (manner) = 3 morphemes.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"morpheme\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"meaning\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F666\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a free morpheme?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Morpheme that stands alone as a word\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Independent unit functioning as complete word. Examples: book, run, blue.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"free morpheme\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"word formation\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F667\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a bound morpheme?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Morpheme that must attach to another\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Cannot stand alone. Must attach to roots. Examples: -ed, un-, -s.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"bound morpheme\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"affixes\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F668\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a root or base?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Main meaning carrier of a word\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Core morpheme with primary meaning. Example: 'struct' in construct, structure, destruction.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"root\",\r\n      \"base\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"word formation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F669\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a prefix?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Morpheme added to word beginning\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Bound morpheme before root. Examples: un- (unhappy), re- (rewrite), pre- (preview).\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"prefix\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"affixes\",\r\n      \"word formation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F670\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a suffix?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Morpheme added to word end\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Bound morpheme after root. Examples: -ness (happiness), -ed (walked), -ly (quickly).\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"suffix\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"affixes\",\r\n      \"word formation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F671\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is inflection?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Grammatical change without new word\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Marks tense, number, comparison. Examples: talk→talked, cat→cats, big→bigger.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"inflection\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"grammar\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F672\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphological concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is derivation?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Creating new word or word class\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Changes meaning or part of speech. Example: happy (adj) → happiness (noun).\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"derivation\",\r\n      \"morphology\",\r\n      \"word formation\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F673\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Lexical and semantic concepts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What is a lexicon?\",\r\n    \"answer\": \"Total vocabulary of language or speaker\",\r\n    \"explanation\": \"Complete word inventory. Example: engineer's lexicon includes torque, gear ratio, voltage.\",\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"tags\": [\r\n      \"lexicon\",\r\n      \"vocabulary\",\r\n      \"semantics\",\r\n      \"linguistics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"mode\": \"Learn\",\r\n    \"type\": \"flashcard\",\r\n    \"options\": []\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"F674\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\"