    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is the rhyme scheme of a Petrarchan (Italian) sonnet, not Shakespearean.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The three quatrains follow alternating rhyme (ABAB, CDCD, EFEF), ending with a rhyming couplet (GG).\",\r\n      \"This pattern uses only couplets, which doesn't match the Shakespearean structure.\",\r\n      \"This pattern has no rhyme scheme at all and isn't a traditional sonnet form.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q113\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Poetry Analysis\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Where does the volta (turn in thought) typically occur in a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Where does the volta (turn in thought) typically occur in a Shakespearean sonnet?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"At line 5\",\r\n      \"At line 9 or in the final couplet (line 13)\",\r\n      \"At the very beginning (line 1)\",\r\n      \"At line 8\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"At line 9 or in the final couplet (line 13)\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Too early. Line 5 is in the middle of the second quatrain, not a typical turning point.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The volta usually appears at the start of the third quatrain (line 9) or in the final couplet (line 13), shifting the poem's direction or offering resolution.\",\r\n      \"The poem typically establishes its theme first before turning, so line 1 is too early.\",\r\n      \"Line 8 is more common in Petrarchan sonnets, not Shakespearean structure.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q114\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-2\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Poetry Analysis\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which of the following is a recurring theme in Shakespeare's sonnets?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following is a recurring theme in Shakespeare's sonnets?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Space exploration and technology\",\r\n      \"Love, time, beauty, and mortality\",\r\n      \"Political revolution\",\r\n      \"Agricultural practices\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Love, time, beauty, and mortality\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Anachronistic. Shakespeare lived in the late 16th/early 17th century, long before space exploration.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Shakespeare's sonnets deeply explore universal themes of love, the passage of time, the nature of beauty, death, and existential questions.\",\r\n      \"While Shakespeare wrote political plays, his sonnets focus more on personal and philosophical themes.\",\r\n      \"Not a central theme in Shakespeare's sonnets, which focus on human emotion and experience.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q115\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What's the primary focus of phonemic awareness?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"What's the primary focus of phonemic awareness?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Recognizing sentence rhythm\",\r\n      \"Recognizing individual sounds in words\",\r\n      \"Identifying prefixes and suffixes\",\r\n      \"Memorizing sight words\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Recognizing individual sounds in words\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Sentence rhythm relates to prosody, not phonemic awareness.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Phonemic awareness focuses on hearing, identifying, and manipulating individual sounds (phonemes) in spoken words.\",\r\n      \"Prefixes and suffixes are part of morphology, not phonemic awareness.\",\r\n      \"Sight word memorization is a separate literacy skill.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q116\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which skill bridges decoding and comprehension?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which skill bridges decoding and comprehension?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Vocabulary development\",\r\n      \"Phoneme blending\",\r\n      \"Sight word recognition\",\r\n      \"Oral fluency\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Oral fluency\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While important, vocabulary is more directly related to comprehension than bridging the two.\",\r\n      \"Phoneme blending is a decoding skill, not a bridge between decoding and comprehension.\",\r\n      \"Sight words support decoding but don't bridge to comprehension.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Oral fluency combines accurate decoding with expression and understanding, linking both skills.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q117\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What stage comes before transitional literacy?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"What stage comes before transitional literacy?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Emergent\",\r\n      \"Fluent\",\r\n      \"Early\",\r\n      \"Proficient\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Early\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Emergent is the first stage, coming before early, not transitional.\",\r\n      \"Fluent comes after transitional.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The progression is: Emergent → Early → Transitional → Fluent → Proficient.\",\r\n      \"Proficient is the final stage, after fluent.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q118\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Why is morphology instruction beneficial?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Why is morphology instruction beneficial?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"It improves handwriting\",\r\n      \"It helps infer meaning from unfamiliar words\",\r\n      \"It improves sentence rhythm\",\r\n      \"It builds phonemic awareness\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"It helps infer meaning from unfamiliar words\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Morphology doesn't relate to handwriting (a motor skill).\",\r\n      \"Correct! Understanding roots, prefixes, and suffixes allows students to decode and infer meaning from new words.\",\r\n      \"Sentence rhythm relates to prosody, not morphology.\",\r\n      \"Phonemic awareness deals with sounds, not word parts.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q119\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What's a key strategy for struggling readers?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"What's a key strategy for struggling readers?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Reducing text exposure\",\r\n      \"Assigning silent reading only\",\r\n      \"Modeling fluent reading\",\r\n      \"Avoiding feedback\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Modeling fluent reading\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Reducing exposure limits practice opportunities and slows progress.\",\r\n      \"Silent reading alone doesn't provide the modeling and support struggling readers need.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Modeling fluent reading demonstrates proper pacing, expression, and decoding strategies.\",\r\n      \"Feedback is essential for growth and correction of errors.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q120\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What's metacognition in literacy?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"What's metacognition in literacy?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Memorizing definitions\",\r\n      \"Monitoring and adjusting one's understanding\",\r\n      \"Identifying literary genres\",\r\n      \"Copying model sentences\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Monitoring and adjusting one's understanding\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Memorization is a surface-level skill, not metacognition.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Metacognition is thinking about one's own thinking—actively monitoring comprehension and adjusting strategies.\",\r\n      \"Genre identification is a separate literacy skill.\",\r\n      \"Copying is a mechanical task, not reflective thinking.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q121\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Why is background knowledge critical for comprehension?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Why is background knowledge critical for comprehension?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"It reduces decoding time\",\r\n      \"It links new information to existing schema\",\r\n      \"It improves phonics speed\",\r\n      \"It supports handwriting fluency\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"It links new information to existing schema\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Background knowledge relates to meaning-making, not decoding speed.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Prior knowledge connects new information to existing understanding (schema), providing context for comprehension.\",\r\n      \"Phonics speed is unrelated to background knowledge.\",\r\n      \"Handwriting is a separate motor skill.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q122\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"How does syntax shape meaning?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"How does syntax shape meaning?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Determines pronunciation\",\r\n      \"Influences rhythm\",\r\n      \"Organizes ideas and emphasis\",\r\n      \"Adds imagery\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Organizes ideas and emphasis\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Pronunciation is determined by phonology, not syntax.\",\r\n      \"While syntax can affect rhythm, its primary function is meaning organization.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Sentence structure organizes ideas, determines emphasis, and affects how information is interpreted.\",\r\n      \"Imagery comes from word choice (diction), not sentence structure.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q123\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What's the main purpose of literacy assessment?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"What's the main purpose of literacy assessment?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Ranking students\",\r\n      \"Identifying learning needs and progress\",\r\n      \"Recording attendance\",\r\n      \"Rewarding effort\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Identifying learning needs and progress\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Ranking can demotivate and doesn't guide instruction effectively.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Assessment diagnoses strengths and needs, guiding targeted instruction and measuring growth.\",\r\n      \"Attendance tracking is administrative, not instructional assessment.\",\r\n      \"While recognition matters, assessment's primary purpose is diagnostic and instructional.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q124\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Developmental Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which element defines fluent reading?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which element defines fluent reading?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Slow, careful decoding\",\r\n      \"Smooth, accurate, expressive reading\",\r\n      \"Memorization of every word\",\r\n      \"Reading silently only\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Smooth, accurate, expressive reading\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Slow, careful reading indicates developing fluency, not fluent reading.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Fluent reading is smooth, accurate, and expressive with appropriate pacing and prosody.\",\r\n      \"Fluency involves automatic decoding, not memorization of all words.\",\r\n      \"Fluency applies to both oral and silent reading.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q125\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Functions in Context\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which best defines language register?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which best defines language register?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Vocabulary level only\",\r\n      \"Sentence structure\",\r\n      \"Level of formality for audience and purpose\",\r\n      \"Tone of voice\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Level of formality for audience and purpose\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Register involves more than just vocabulary—it includes style and formality across all language elements.\",\r\n      \"Sentence structure is one component but doesn't fully define register.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Language register is the degree of formality adjusted for specific contexts, audiences, and purposes.\",\r\n      \"Tone is related but distinct from register; register encompasses broader stylistic choices.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q126\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Functions in Context\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What does code-switching demonstrate?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"What does code-switching demonstrate?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Poor grammar use\",\r\n      \"Flexibility in language based on audience\",\r\n      \"Avoidance of cultural norms\",\r\n      \"Memorization of word roots\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Flexibility in language based on audience\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Code-switching is a sophisticated linguistic skill, not poor grammar.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Code-switching shows linguistic flexibility and cultural awareness by adapting language to different situations and audiences.\",\r\n      \"Code-switching actually demonstrates awareness and respect for cultural norms.\",\r\n      \"Word roots relate to morphology, not code-switching.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q127\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Functions in Context\",\r\n    \"question\": \"What branch of linguistics studies meaning in context?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"What branch of linguistics studies meaning in context?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Phonology\",\r\n      \"Morphology\",\r\n      \"Pragmatics\",\r\n      \"Semantics\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Pragmatics\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Phonology studies sound systems in language.\",\r\n      \"Morphology examines word structure and formation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Pragmatics analyzes how context, speaker intent, and social factors influence meaning.\",\r\n      \"Semantics studies meaning but doesn't emphasize context as pragmatics does.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q128\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Functions in Context\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which sentence shows positive connotation?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence shows positive connotation?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"He's cheap.\",\r\n      \"He's thrifty.\",\r\n      \"He's stingy.\",\r\n      \"He's miserly.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"He's thrifty.\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"'Cheap' has negative connotations of low quality or excessive frugality.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Thrifty' has positive connotations of wise money management.\",\r\n      \"'Stingy' has negative connotations of unwillingness to spend or share.\",\r\n      \"'Miserly' has strongly negative connotations of excessive hoarding.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q129\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Functions in Context\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Denotation refers to a word's…\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Denotation refers to a word's…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Historical origin\",\r\n      \"Literal meaning\",\r\n      \"Figurative association\",\r\n      \"Emotional tone\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Literal meaning\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Etymology studies historical origins, not denotation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Denotation is the dictionary definition or literal meaning of a word.\",\r\n      \"Figurative associations relate to connotation, not denotation.\",\r\n      \"Emotional tone is part of connotation, not denotation.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q130\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which is NOT a key component of communication?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which is NOT a key component of communication?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Sender\",\r\n      \"Message\",\r\n      \"Grammar\",\r\n      \"Feedback\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Grammar\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The sender initiates communication.\",\r\n      \"The message is the content being communicated.\",\r\n      \"Correct! While grammar aids clarity, it's not one of the six essential components of the communication process.\",\r\n      \"Feedback allows the sender to gauge message reception.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q131\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which quality ensures effective communication?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which quality ensures effective communication?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Ambiguity\",\r\n      \"Clarity\",\r\n      \"Volume\",\r\n      \"Length\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Clarity\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Ambiguity obscures meaning and hinders communication.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Clarity ensures the message is understood as intended.\",\r\n      \"Volume is a delivery factor but doesn't guarantee effective communication.\",\r\n      \"Length doesn't ensure effectiveness; conciseness is often preferable.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q132\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Tone is defined as…\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Tone is defined as…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The emotional quality or attitude in language\",\r\n      \"The complexity of sentence structure\",\r\n      \"The literal meaning of a word\",\r\n      \"The rhythm of speech\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"The emotional quality or attitude in language\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Tone reflects the speaker's or writer's attitude toward the subject and audience.\",\r\n      \"Sentence complexity relates to syntax, not tone.\",\r\n      \"Literal meaning is denotation, not tone.\",\r\n      \"Rhythm relates to prosody, not tone (though they can work together).\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q133\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Active listening means…\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Active listening means…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Hearing and ignoring irrelevant details\",\r\n      \"Responding thoughtfully and showing understanding\",\r\n      \"Taking notes only\",\r\n      \"Finishing sentences for the speaker\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Responding thoughtfully and showing understanding\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Active listening requires engagement with all