ses that clarify logic\",\r\n      \"Removing topic sentences\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Adding transitional phrases that clarify logic\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Font variation is visual, not logical coherence.\",\r\n      \"Slang reduces formality and precision, not improves coherence.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Transitions (however, therefore, consequently) clarify logical relationships between ideas.\",\r\n      \"Topic sentences guide readers—removing them reduces coherence.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q416\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"A business letter should prioritize:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A business letter should prioritize:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Personal storytelling and figurative language\",\r\n      \"Casual tone and slang\",\r\n      \"Clear purpose, concise paragraphs, formal register\",\r\n      \"Second-person commands throughout\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Clear purpose, concise paragraphs, formal register\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Business letters should be professional, not personal or figurative.\",\r\n      \"Casual tone/slang lacks professionalism.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Business letters require clarity, brevity, formal register, and professional tone.\",\r\n      \"Commands can be appropriate but aren't the priority—clarity and formality are.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q417\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-3\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Structure, Literary Response, and Expository Writing Techniques\",\r\n    \"question\": \"In an expository essay, which evidence is MOST appropriate to support a data-heavy claim?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"In an expository essay, which evidence is MOST appropriate to support a data-heavy claim?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Vivid sensory description\",\r\n      \"Peer-reviewed research and credible statistics\",\r\n      \"Personal anecdote only\",\r\n      \"Fictional dialogue\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Peer-reviewed research and credible statistics\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Sensory description is literary, not analytical evidence.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Data-heavy claims require credible, factual evidence from reliable sources.\",\r\n      \"Personal anecdotes alone lack the authority of research.\",\r\n      \"Fictional dialogue is creative, not factual evidence.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q601\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Using surrounding sentences to infer an unfamiliar word's meaning demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Using surrounding sentences to infer an unfamiliar word's meaning demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Phonetic decoding\",\r\n      \"Context clue analysis\",\r\n      \"Syntax identification\",\r\n      \"Summarizing\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Context clue analysis\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Phonetic decoding uses sound-letter relationships, not context.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Context clue analysis uses surrounding words and sentences to determine meaning.\",\r\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not meaning inference.\",\r\n      \"Summarizing condenses main ideas, not word meanings.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\