tials\",\r\n      \"Date of publication\",\r\n      \"Evidence citations\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Design aesthetics\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! While design matters for usability, aesthetics don't determine credibility or accuracy.\",\r\n      \"Author credentials indicate expertise and credibility—essential.\",\r\n      \"Publication date shows currency—important for time-sensitive topics.\",\r\n      \"Evidence citations demonstrate research quality—essential.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q624\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"The purpose of a public service announcement is primarily to:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The purpose of a public service announcement is primarily to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Entertain\",\r\n      \"Inform or persuade\",\r\n      \"Amuse\",\r\n      \"Narrate\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Inform or persuade\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"PSAs can engage but entertainment isn't the primary purpose.\",\r\n      \"Correct! PSAs inform audiences about issues and/or persuade them toward action or behavior change.\",\r\n      \"Amusement may be a technique but not the goal.\",\r\n      \"Narration is a method, not a purpose.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q625\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"A speaker using celebrity endorsements to influence an audience uses which propaganda technique?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A speaker using celebrity endorsements to influence an audience uses which propaganda technique?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Transfer\",\r\n      \"Testimonial\",\r\n      \"Plain folks\",\r\n      \"Fear appeal\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Testimonial\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Transfer associates with symbols/concepts, not specific endorsers.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Testimonial uses endorsements from individuals (especially celebrities) to build credibility.\",\r\n      \"Plain folks presents speaker as ordinary person, opposite of celebrity.\",\r\n      \"Fear appeal uses threats or warnings, not endorsements.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q626\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Recognizing that film uses close-up shots to create empathy demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Recognizing that film uses close-up shots to create empathy demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Critical media analysis\",\r\n      \"Visual confusion\",\r\n      \"Phonemic awareness\",\r\n      \"Literal comprehension\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Critical media analysis\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Critical media analysis examines how technical choices (like shot selection) shape meaning and emotion.\",\r\n      \"This shows understanding, not confusion.\",\r\n      \"Phonemic awareness involves sound manipulation, not film analysis.\",\r\n      \"Literal comprehension understands surface meaning, not analyzes technique.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q627\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-4\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies, Critical Analysis, Reader Response, Cross-Curricular Writing, and Media Literacy\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Identifying how background music intensifies a news segment's emotional tone analyzes:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Identifying how background music intensifies a news segment's emotional tone analyzes:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Nonverbal cues in media\",\r\n      \"Logical appeals\",\r\n      \"Morphological structure\",\r\n      \"Syntax\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Nonverbal cues in media\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Nonverbal cues in media include music, sound effects, and visuals that shape emotional response.\",\r\n      \"Logical appeals use reasoning, not music.\",\r\n      \"Morphological structure relates to word formation, not media.\",\r\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not media production.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q628\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"A middle school teacher has students read excerpts from a famous speech before writing their own persuasive paragraphs on a modern topic. To strengthen the connection between reading and writing, the teacher asks students to analyze the rhetorical techniques used in the speech before they draft their own work. The primary benefit of this activity is that it helps students:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A middle school teacher has students read excerpts from a famous speech before writing their own persuasive paragraphs on a modern topic. To strengthen the connection between reading and writing, the teacher asks students to analyze the rhetorical techniques used in the speech before they draft their own work. The primary benefit of this activity is that it helps students:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"memorize formal patterns of written and spoken argument.\",\r\n      \"transfer comprehension strategies from reading into composition.\",\r\n      \"imitate the stylistic conventions of historical texts.\",\r\n      \"focus on correct spelling and punctuation while writing.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"transfer comprehension strategies from reading into composition.\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Memorization is not the focus; understanding and application of rhetorical techniques is the goal.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Students are connecting analytical reading (how rhetoric works) with their own writing decisions—an integrated literacy practice.\",\r\n      \"Imitation is not the main goal; transferring analytical skills to composition is the focus.\",\r\n      \"Spelling and punctuation are not the primary focus of analyzing rhetorical techniques.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q629\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"After studying short stories with strong character development, students are asked to perform short dramatic readings of key scenes before writing a reflective essay about character motivation. The main advantage of this sequence is that it enables students to:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"After studying short stories with strong character development, students are asked to perform short dramatic readings of key scenes before writing a reflective essay about character motivation. The main advantage of this sequence is that it enables students to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"compare film and literature as forms of storytelling.\",\r\n      \"understand narrative pacing through silent reading.\",\r\n      \"internalize literary characterization through expressive performance.\",\r\n      \"demonstrate memorization skills for key literary passages.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"internalize literary characterization through expressive performance.\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Film comparison is not part of this activity; the focus is on dramatic reading and writing.\",\r\n      \"Silent reading is not the method used here; students are performing aloud.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Oral interpretation deepens comprehension and helps students feel how language shapes character and tone.\",\r\n      \"Memorization is not the goal; understanding character motivation through performance is the focus.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q630\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"A teacher plays an audio recording of a contemporary poet reading their own work, then asks students to analyze how the poet's vocal delivery affects meaning. Students then record themselves reading one of their own poems aloud. This assignment primarily promotes students' ability to:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher plays an audio recording of a contemporary poet reading their own work, then asks students to analyze how the poet's vocal delivery affects meaning. Students then record themselves reading one of their own poems aloud. This assignment primarily promotes students' ability to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"evaluate how spoken language choices convey nuance and emotion.\",\r\n      \"identify phonemic awareness in a literary context.\",\r\n      \"assess the poet's adherence to standard pronunciation norms.\",\r\n      \"memorize and recite poetry for performance competition.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"evaluate how spoken language choices convey nuance and emotion.\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! It links listening and speaking to interpretation—core ELA integration.\",\r\n      \"Phonemic awareness relates to sound manipulation in language learning, not poetry analysis.\",\r\n      \"Standard pronunciation is not the focus; vocal delivery and meaning are the goals.\",\r\n      \"Memorization and competition are not the objectives; analysis and self-expression are the focus.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q631\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Students in an English class maintain a weekly blog where they respond to class readings and comment on one another's posts. This activity most directly supports development in which area?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Students in an English class maintain a weekly blog where they respond to class readings and comment on one another's posts. This activity most directly supports development in which area?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"grammar and sentence mechanics\",\r\n      \"collaborative writing and audience awareness\",\r\n      \"historical literary analysis\",\r\n      \"memorization of key vocabulary terms\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"collaborative writing and audience awareness\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While grammar may be practiced, it is not the primary focus of blogging and commenting.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Commenting and responding online merges reading, writing, and communication skills while reinforcing authentic audience awareness.\",\r\n      \"Historical literary analysis may occur but is not the main benefit of the blog activity.\",\r\n      \"Memorization is not a focus; the activity emphasizes interaction and communication.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q632\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\r\n    \"question\": \"An English teacher asks students to create short podcasts explaining the themes of assigned novels and to include brief quotations to support their points. This approach best fosters:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An English teacher asks students to create short podcasts explaining the themes of assigned novels and to include brief quotations to support their points. This approach best fosters:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"oral fluency through repeated reading practice.\",\r\n      \"integrated comprehension by synthesizing speaking, reading, and writing skills.\",\r\n      \"technical editing of recorded audio files.\",\r\n      \"memorization of literary quotations for assessment purposes.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"integrated comprehension by synthesizing speaking, reading, and writing skills.\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Oral fluency may improve, but the main goal is integrating multiple literacy skills.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Students read to gather evidence, write to plan their script, and speak to communicate analysis—pure language arts integration.\",\r\n      \"Technical editing is a side skill, not the primary educational focus.\",\r\n      \"Memorization is not the goal; synthesis and communication of ideas are the focus.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q633\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"question\": \"During the Old English period, the language was most heavily influenced by:\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"During the Old English period, the language was most heavily influenced by:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Latin brought by Roman missionaries\",\r\n      \"Norse vocabulary introduced through Viking contact\",\r\n      \"Celtic grammatical structures\",\r\n      \"Norman French syntax\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Norse vocabulary introduced through Viking contact\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While Latin influenced Old English through Christianity, it was not the most pervasive influence.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The Viking invasions infused Old English with Norse loanwords like 'sky,' 'egg,' and 'window,' shaping northern dialects.\",\r\n      \"Celtic had minimal impact on Old English grammar; most Celtic influence was place names.\",\r\n      \"Norman French came later during the Middle English period, not Old English.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q634\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which event most contributed to the standardization of English spelling and grammar?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which event most contributed to the standardization of English spelling and grammar?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The Great Vowel Shift\",\r\n      \"The introduction of the printing press by William Caxton in 1476\",\r\n      \"The Protestant Reformation\",\r\n      \"The publication of Johnson's Dictionary of the English Language in 1755\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"The introduction of the printing press by William Caxton in 1476\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The Great Vowel Shift changed pronunciation but did not standardize spelling.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The printing press made written English more uniform and spread London's dialect as a standard.\",\r\n      \"The Reformation influenced Bible translation but was not the primary standardization force.\",\r\n      \"Johnson's Dictionary was important but came after printing had already established many standards.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q635\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which development most clearly reflects the influence of global expansion on the English language?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which development most clearly reflects the influence of global expansion on the English language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The adoption of words like café and rendezvous\",\r\n      \"The inclusion of loanwords from Hindi, Arabic, and Swahili\",\r\n      \"The simplification of English verb conjugations\",\r\n      \"The creation of Middle English poetic forms\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"The inclusion of loanwords from Hindi, Arabic, and Swahili\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"French borrowings reflect European contact, not global expansion.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Colonial contact introduced vocabulary such as 'bungalow,' 'algebra,' and 'safari,' demonstrating English's global borrowing.\",\r\n      \"Verb simplification was an internal development, not due to global expansion.\",\r\n      \"Middle English poetry was a literary development, not related to global contact.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q636\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which factor most contributed to the divergence of British and American English spelling conventions?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which factor most contributed to the divergence of British and American English spelling conventions?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The establishment of colonial universities\",\r\n      \"Noah Webster's dictionary reforms\",\r\n      \"The Industrial Revolution\",\r\n      \"Early missionary translation projects\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Noah Webster's dictionary reforms\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Colonial universities preserved British conventions rather than diverging from them.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Webster intentionally simplified spellings—'color' vs. 'colour'—to promote linguistic independence in the new nation.\",\r\n      \"The Industrial Revolution affected vocabulary but not systematic spelling differences.\",\r\n      \"Missionary projects did not focus on spelling reform.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"mode\": \"Drill\",\r\n    \"type\": \"mcq\"\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q637\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"subareaId\": \"SA-1\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"question\": \"Which of the following best explains the rapid expansion of new vocabulary during the 20th century?\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following best explains the rapid expansion of new vocabulary during the 20th century?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Growth of literacy and public schooling\",\r\n      \"Technological and scientific innovation\",\r\n      \"Simplification of English grammar\",\r\n      \"Increased translation of classical texts\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"answer\": \"Technological and scientific innovation\",\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Literacy growth helped spread language but did not create new vocabulary.\",\